@*
 * FILE ROLE: Root Home view — the application entry point at /. Demonstrates all
 *   three forms of area navigation: asp-area tag helper, @Url.Action with area,
 *   and a table summarising cross-area link patterns.
 *
 * SECTION 12: ROOT VIEW — CROSS-AREA NAVIGATION
 *
 * This view renders inside Views/Shared/_Layout.cshtml (set by Views/_ViewStart.cshtml).
 * It is a non-area view: no [Area] context is active, so the asp-area tag helper
 * must explicitly name the target area for every link to an area.
 *
 * THREE WAYS TO LINK TO AN AREA ACTION:
 *
 *   1. asp-area TAG HELPER (recommended — declarative, compile-time route validation)
 *      <a asp-area="Admin"    asp-controller="Dashboard" asp-action="Index">Admin</a>
 *      <a asp-area="Customer" asp-controller="Account"   asp-action="Profile">Customer</a>
 *      <a asp-area=""         asp-controller="Home"      asp-action="Index">Home</a>
 *
 *   2. @Url.Action (useful when href is needed as a string, e.g. in JS or redirects)
 *      @Url.Action("Index",   "Dashboard", new { area = "Admin" })
 *      @Url.Action("Profile", "Account",   new { area = "Customer" })
 *      @Url.Action("Index",   "Home",      new { area = "" })
 *
 *   3. @Html.ActionLink (legacy helper — prefer tag helpers in new code)
 *      @Html.ActionLink("Admin", "Index", "Dashboard", new { area = "Admin" }, null)
 *
 * AMBIENT AREA VALUE TRAP:
 *   When using asp-area without a value, the anchor tag helper uses the ambient
 *   area from the current request's route data. From a root (non-area) view the
 *   ambient area is empty, so omitting asp-area would work here. BUT in a shared
 *   layout or partial that can be rendered from both area and non-area contexts,
 *   omitting asp-area leads to incorrect hrefs from area pages.
 *   Best practice: always specify asp-area explicitly when writing links.
*@

@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Home";
}

<h1>ASP.NET Core MVC — Areas</h1>
<p>This is the root <strong>Home/Index</strong> view, served by
   <code>Controllers/HomeController.cs</code> via the default (non-area) route.</p>

<h2>Navigate to Areas</h2>
<table>
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Destination</th>
            <th>Tag helper (asp-area)</th>
            <th>Generated URL</th>
            <th><code>Url.Action</code> equivalent</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td>Admin Dashboard</td>
            <td>
                @* asp-area="Admin" directs the route to the Admin area *@
                <a asp-area="Admin" asp-controller="Dashboard" asp-action="Index">Admin Dashboard</a>
            </td>
            <td><code>/Admin/Dashboard/Index</code></td>
            <td><code>@Url.Action("Index", "Dashboard", new { area = "Admin" })</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Customer Profile</td>
            <td>
                @* asp-area="Customer" directs the route to the Customer area *@
                <a asp-area="Customer" asp-controller="Account" asp-action="Profile">Customer Profile</a>
            </td>
            <td><code>/Customer/Account/Profile</code></td>
            <td><code>@Url.Action("Profile", "Account", new { area = "Customer" })</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Home (self)</td>
            <td>
                @* asp-area="" explicitly clears area context → non-area route *@
                <a asp-area="" asp-controller="Home" asp-action="Index">Home</a>
            </td>
            <td><code>/</code></td>
            <td><code>@Url.Action("Index", "Home", new { area = "" })</code></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2>About This Project</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Root <code>Controllers/HomeController.cs</code> — no [Area] attribute, reached via default route</li>
    <li><code>Areas/Admin/Controllers/DashboardController.cs</code> — [Area("Admin")], own layout</li>
    <li><code>Areas/Customer/Controllers/AccountController.cs</code> — [Area("Customer")], uses root layout</li>
</ul>
